---
import {
  BRAND_LEAD,
  BRAND_CORE,
  BRAND_KIND,
  SCORE_SHORT,
  REPORT_NAME,
} from "../lib/brand";
import EditionMark from "./EditionMark.astro";
import ScoreLadder from "./ScoreLadder.astro";
import CategoryExplainer from "./CategoryExplainer.astro";
import ScoreName from "./ScoreName.astro";

/**
 * THE SPLASH.
 *
 * What changed and why. The first build was a centred stack over four identical cards, which is a
 * named anti-pattern (same-size boxes, heading plus two lines, repeated) and reads as a template
 * before a word is read. Three moves replace it:
 *
 *   1 · ASYMMETRY. The headline holds the left, the instrument holds the right. Nothing is centred.
 *   2 · THE REWARD IS ON SCREEN ONE. The maturity meter renders here with no needle, captioned as
 *       what the respondent leaves with. A promise you can see costs less belief than one you read.
 *   3 · THE BENEFITS ARE A RAIL, NOT CARDS. Four statements divided by hairlines and numbered. A
 *       rule groups as well as a box and promises less, which matters when three of the four are
 *       still deferred promises.
 *
 * COPY: Dan's strings, with one class of edit. Em dashes are banned outright by DESIGN.md ("use
 * commas, colons, semicolons, periods or parentheses"), so two of them become full stops. Nothing
 * else in the wording moves.
 */

/**
 * WHAT THE RESPONDENT GETS, AND WHEN.
 *
 * The `when` line is not decoration, it is the missing information. Three of these four are
 * deferred promises and one lands before the respondent closes the tab, and a rail that does not
 * say so is asking to be read as four equivalent offers. The badge says it, in words, on every
 * card. It is the ONLY thing that distinguishes them, which is deliberate: an earlier build also
 * drew the immediate one in the accent — its own rule, numeral, title, badge and ink — and Oleh
 * took the whole treatment off on 2026-08-26. A permanent highlight claims one of four is current,
 * nothing here is current, and the accent is the colour this page uses for things you can click.
 * The difference is information, so the difference is carried by words. See `.bo-rail__item--now`
 * in survey.css, which is now a note explaining its own absence.
 *
 * STILL NO ICONS, BUT NOW THERE IS ART. An icon above each of four equal-sized blocks is the
 * template pattern `impeccable` bans by name (identical card grids; rounded icons above every
 * heading), and that ban is untouched: the badge still carries a word, so the item survives the
 * Never-Alone Rule without a glyph to lean on.
 *
 * What changed on 2026-08-26 is the scope. Oleh lifted PRODUCT.md's rejection of decorative
 * gradients and illustration FOR THIS APP ONLY: the survey is an independent marketing surface,
 * closer to a website than to the admin tool, and it is allowed to look like one. So each item
 * gains a line drawing that sits in a band at its foot, behind nothing, dissolving upward before
 * it reaches a word. It is `aria-hidden` and it is not an icon: it carries no information the
 * title and body do not already state, it is not a label, and removing it removes nothing but
 * atmosphere. `--rail-art-h` in survey.css gives it its own space so it never shares a row with
 * text. The recipe is documented at `.bo-rail__art`.
 */
/**
 * THE ORDER, AND THE ONE BADGE THAT WAS NOT TRUE.
 *
 * Reordered 2026-08-26. The band now leads and the report follows it. The rail is read left to
 * right and the first thing in it should be the thing that actually happens first: the band is the
 * only one of the four that lands before the respondent closes the tab, and it was sitting second,
 * behind a promise that resolves whenever the report ships. Leading with the deferred one asks for
 * belief before it has given anything. The numerals are `i + 1`, so 01/02/03/04 follow the array
 * and there is no second place to keep in sync.
 *
 * The band's badge read `IN TEN MINUTES`, which was borrowed from the wrong clock. Ten minutes is
 * how long the SURVEY takes — it is on the meta row above as `~10 min` and in the meter's caption —
 * and reusing it as the band's DELIVERY time says the band arrives ten minutes from now, at the end
 * of a wait, which is not what happens. There is no wait: the band is drawn from answers already
 * given, so it is there as the last one is saved. `THE MOMENT YOU FINISH` is both shorter and true,
 * and it stops the same number meaning two things on one screen.
 *
 * The band's body then had to give up its first clause, which said "The moment you finish" in
 * full — with the badge carrying it, the sentence was repeating its own label back at the reader.
 * The rest of the line is untouched, and the other three are not edited at all.
 */

const BENEFITS = [
  {
    when: "The moment you finish",
    art: "band",
    /* THE ONLY TITLE THAT IS NOT A PLAIN STRING. It names the score, so it wants the acronym
       marked, and a data string cannot carry markup. `scored` is the flag the template reads;
       `title` stays as the accessible plain-text fallback and as the thing to edit if the wording
       changes. See `ScoreName.astro`. */
    scored: true,
    title: `Your ${SCORE_SHORT}`,
    body: "A number out of 100 and the band it puts you in. No waiting for the report.",
  },
  {
    when: "When it ships",
    art: "report",
    title: "The report, first",
    body: `You get ${REPORT_NAME} before anyone else, and see how your practices compare.`,
  },
  {
    when: "At launch",
    art: "early",
    title: "Early access",
    body: "Respondents are first in line when Baseout opens up, with an early access discount.",
  },
  {
    when: "Agencies/Partners",
    art: "partner",
    title: "Founding partner status",
    body: "Agencies and consultants can join the Baseout partner program at founding level, with founding perks.",
  },
];
---

<div class="bo-split splash-split">
  {
    /* THE EYEBROW IS ITS OWN GRID ROW, and that is the whole alignment mechanism. It used to sit
       inside the left column, which meant the split had ONE row whose top was the eyebrow's top —
       so anything placed at the top of the RIGHT column started level with the eyebrow, two rungs
       above where the headline starts. Lifting it into a row of its own makes row 2 open exactly
       where the `h1` opens, in both columns at once, and the lockup below is then level with the
       headline because of where it sits rather than because an offset keeps it there.

       It moves nothing on screen: `row-gap` is zeroed for this split, so the 0.5rem the eyebrow
       already carried as its own `margin-bottom` is still the only thing between it and the `h1`. */
  }
  <p class="bo-eyebrow splash-eyebrow">The first-ever</p>

  <div class="splash-head">
    {
      /* THE NAME IS ASSEMBLED, NOT TYPED. `BRAND` carries no platform, so the Notion run is one
            constant away. See `lib/brand.ts`. */
    }
    {
      /* THE EDITION IS SAID ONCE, AND NOT HERE. The standing lockup in the right column sets
         "Airtable edition" in full, so repeating it in the headline made the page say the same two
         words twice before it said anything else — Oleh, 2026-08-28. The headline stops at the
         brand, which is also the thing that carries to the Notion run: `BRAND` holds no platform.

         The word does not go missing: the lede below names Airtable in its first line, and
         `<title>` is built from `EDITION`, so neither search nor a shared link loses the platform. */
    }
    {
      /* THREE PARTS, TWO VOLUMES. Dan, 2026-08-28: *"maybe we differentiate with a different font,
         or a different colour — 'State of', and then 'Survey' below, separated from the name."*

         The split is not decoration; it is the one place on the site that shows which words are the
         coined thing. "State of" and "Survey" are what every industry benchmark is called. "DevOps
         for App Platforms" is the part Dan named and the part that has to be remembered, so it
         keeps the display weight and the outer two drop to `--soft` (weight 300, muted ink). Same
         face, same size, same line — one voice at two volumes, which is the contrast this app
         already uses and is quieter than the second typeface he floated.

         EACH PART IS ITS OWN LINE, because that is what "Survey below, separated from the name"
         asks for. The `<br>`s are gone with them: the three spans are blocks, so the headline breaks
         where the name breaks and not where the column happens to run out. `bo-display` sets
         `line-height: 0.95`, so this changes the rag, not the leading.

         It is still ONE `h1` and one accessible name — the spans carry no roles and no aria, so a
         screen reader reads "State of DevOps for App Platforms Survey" straight through. */
    }
    <h1 class="bo-display splash-title">
      <span class="bo-display__soft">{BRAND_LEAD}</span>
      <span class="splash-title__core">{BRAND_CORE}</span>
      <span class="bo-display__soft">{BRAND_KIND}</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="bo-lede bo-measure">
      How do teams actually protect, manage, and govern the Airtable bases their
      business runs on? Nobody has measured it. Help us build the benchmark.
    </p>
    {
      /*
        ── ONE BUTTON. THERE IS NO SECOND DOOR ON THIS PAGE ──────────────────────────────────────

        This was a pair: `Get your DOAP Score` and `Resume your survey`, side by side, equal weight.
        Dan, 2026-08-28, and he could not finish the thought: *"the thing that I struggle with is
        this one right here — there's two calls to action, one is 'get your DOAP score', the other
        is like 'take the survey'. And they both, you know, you kind of get both out of them. So…
        not sure."*

        WHAT HE FELT AND DID NOT NAME: they are not two choices. They are one action and one
        recovery path, presented as equals. Read what each actually does and there is nobody the
        pair serves —

          first visit          nothing to resume; the second button is noise
          returning, same box  `/survey` already resumes on its own (`survey.astro`, `init()`), so
                               the button is noise here too
          returning, new box   the button works — and the start gate, one screen later, already
                               offers "Already started on another device? Get a resume link"

        So the second door duplicated a link on the next screen, for the one case the automatic
        path does not cover, while charging every first-time reader a decision to ignore it. The
        landing page has exactly one job and two equal objects at the top split it in half.

        A CONDITIONAL LINK WAS BUILT HERE FIRST AND TAKEN OUT THE SAME HOUR. It was `hidden` until
        `localStorage` proved this browser held answers, which is defensible and still added a
        second thing to read under the one button that matters. Oleh, 2026-08-31, looking at it:
        *"let's drop 'continue your survey' from the home page entirely. We'll sort it out there in
        practice — let that link live only inside, on the first screen where we ask for the email."*
        He is right that the recovery path belongs at the door it recovers, not on the poster.

        NOTHING IS LOST. The resume link is on the start gate, one click away, where a returning
        reader is already being asked to identify themselves — and on the same device they never
        reach it, because `/survey` resumes them before the gate renders.

        WHY THE BUTTON NAMES THE SCORE. Dan read the pair as reward against labour ("one is get your
        DOAP score, the other is take the survey"). With one button it has to be one or the other,
        and it names the reward: nobody is misled, because the headline ends in "Survey" and the
        fact row below says "~15 min", and the coined term needs the repetition.
      */
    }
    <div class="splash-cta">
      <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--primary" href="/survey">Get your {SCORE_SHORT}</a
      >
    </div>
    {
      /* Four facts, short enough to hold one line: a wrapped row leaves a rule hanging at the
            break, and the separator then reads as a missing item rather than a divider. */
    }
    <ul class="bo-meta">
      <li>~15 min</li>
      <li>Saves as you go</li>
      <li>No account</li>
      <li>Aggregate only</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {
    /*
        The instrument, unresolved. Same component as the one on /thanks, minus the needle: this is
        the measurement the respondent is about to take, shown before they take it.
      */
  }
  <div class="splash-meter">
    {
      /* PLACEMENT B LIVES HERE NOW. It used to be rendered by `index.astro` as a flex child of
         `main` asking for the page's right edge, which put it in the page CORNER under the review
         chips — the thing Dan asked to have taken out of the corner. It is a column-mate of the
         ladder instead: same left edge, same column, above it, and level with the headline's first
         line because it is the first thing in row 2 of the split. */
    }
    <div class="splash-edition">
      <EditionMark />
    </div>
    <div class="splash-meter__instrument">
    {
      /* `demo` — the ladder demonstrates itself. Dan, 2026-08-28: the static track *"is just a
         rectangle and a little square"*. It is a THIRD state of the component, not a variation on
         the finish page's `scored` one: nothing here states a number, so nothing here may come to
         rest on one. See `ScoreLadder.astro` and the `.bo-ladder--demo` block in `survey.css`. */
    }
    <ScoreLadder note="0 to 100, four bands" demo>
      <Fragment slot="eyebrow">
        Where your <ScoreName /> lands
      </Fragment>
    </ScoreLadder>
    {
      /* THE SCALE SAYS WHAT IT IS. It was an unnamed axis with four band names under it, and
         Oleh's note on 2026-08-27 was that nothing on it tied it to the score the button beside
         it is selling. The name is on the label above and again in the first line here, which is
         where there is room to say what the number actually measures. */
    }
    <p class="splash-meter__caption">
      <ScoreName article /> puts a number on how a team protects, changes and documents
      the bases it runs on. Most teams have never been placed on this scale, because
      until now there was nothing to place them against. Fifteen minutes of answers
      is what it takes to read your own position.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{
  /*
    THE EXPLAINER, BETWEEN THE HERO AND THE RAIL.

    Where the client put it, and the placement is the argument: the hero states what the survey is
    and the rail states what you get for taking it, and between those two the reader has one
    question the page was not answering — what the thing being measured actually is. It belongs
    before the reward, not after it.

    IT IS NOT BEHIND ANY SWITCH. `?logo=` was a review affordance for one asset in the headline
    block; this section has nothing to do with it and renders identically either way, so it sits
    outside the conditional rather than being duplicated inside both arms of it.

    NOTHING IS STYLED FOR IT HERE, and nothing should be. `CategoryExplainer.astro` is
    self-contained — its own scoped styles, no props, no client script — and a rule written in this
    file would carry THIS component's `data-astro-cid-*` and never reach inside it anyway.

    THE SEAM IS ON THE RHYTHM, MEASURED. `main` is a flex column, so adjacent margins ADD rather
    than collapsing, and stacking two large ones here was the thing to check. It comes out clean:
    the section carries `margin-top: var(--bo-space-6)` (4.5rem, three rhythms) and no bottom
    margin, and `.splash-rail-h` below carries `var(--bo-space-6)` on top. So the split closes,
    three rhythms, the section, three rhythms, the rail heading — the same gap the split and the
    rail heading had between them before this section existed. Nothing to compensate for.
  */
}
<CategoryExplainer />

{
  /*
      THE SECTION HEADING, RESTORED. An earlier build carried "What you get" over these four; it was
      dropped when the cards became a rail, on the theory that four numbered statements under a
      hairline announce themselves. They do not. Coming off the meter's caption, the rail's first
      visible element is the numeral `01`, and a numeral with no antecedent is a puzzle before it is
      a list — the reader has to get to the second card before the set is legible as a set.

      It is an `h2`, and it is the reason the four item titles below are now `h3`: the rail is a
      section of this page and its items are inside it, so the outline has to say so. Nothing about
      them changes visually — every rail rule is written against `.bo-rail__title`, not the tag.

      Size, deliberately: `--bo-t-24`, the same rung `/thanks` gives its own section heading, which
      is a full rung and a fluid clamp below the `h1` above it. This heading opens a section; it
      does not compete for the page.
    */
}
<h2 class="splash-rail-h">What you get</h2>

<ul class="bo-rail splash-rail">
  {
    BENEFITS.map((b, i) => (
      <li class="bo-rail__item">
        {/* Decoration, and nothing else. Hidden from the accessibility tree because the title
              and body already say what it draws, so announcing it would only add a second, vaguer
              copy of the same sentence. */}
        <span class="bo-rail__art" data-art={b.art} aria-hidden="true" />
        <span class="bo-rail__n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
        <span class="bo-badge bo-badge--later">{b.when}</span>
        <h3 class="bo-rail__title">
          {"scored" in b ? <Fragment>Your <ScoreName /></Fragment> : b.title}
        </h3>
        <p class="bo-rail__body">{b.body}</p>
      </li>
    ))
  }
</ul>

<style>
  /* PAGE PLACEMENT ONLY. Every surface, colour, radius and type size comes from a `survey.css`
     recipe; what is left here is where this page puts things. */
  .splash-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--bo-space-2);
    margin-top: var(--bo-space-4);
  }

  /* ── THE SPLIT'S ROWS ─ where the Airtable lockup gets its alignment from ────────────────────
     Two rows, and the eyebrow has row 1 to itself. Row 2 therefore opens at the top of the `h1`
     in BOTH columns, which is the whole reason the lockup in the right column can be level with
     the headline without an offset holding it there.

     `row-gap: 0`, deliberately and in both layouts. `.bo-split`'s `gap` is shorthand and sets both
     axes; with one row that only ever showed as a column gap, and adding a second row would have
     dropped 4.5rem between the eyebrow and the headline. Zeroing the row gap leaves the eyebrow's
     own `margin-bottom` as the only thing between them, which is what it was before. It also
     zeroes the 3rem the collapsed layout was using to separate the two stacked halves, so that
     3rem comes back as an explicit `margin-top` on the meter in the media query below. */
  .splash-split {
    /* The anchor the stacked lockup is pinned to. Without it the lockup positions against the
       initial containing block and lands on the VIEWPORT edge rather than the content edge — which
       is what it did on the first attempt at this. */
    position: relative;
    grid-template-rows: auto auto;
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  .splash-eyebrow {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .splash-head {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 2;
  }

  /* ── THE HEADLINE'S THREE PARTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The two quiet ones are `.bo-display__soft` from `survey.css`, which already carries the block
     display and the weight/colour drop. Only the loud middle is declared here, and only to become
     a block: it inherits everything else from `.bo-display`, so if the display recipe moves, the
     name moves with it and the frame keeps following.

     `text-wrap: balance` is deliberately NOT set. The break points are the three parts, which is
     the point of the split; letting the browser rebalance would put "Survey" back on the name's
     line at some widths and undo the separation Dan asked for. */
  /* BLUE AS WELL AS BOLD, since 2026-08-31. Oleh: *"let's make 'DevOps for App Platforms' blue on
     the home screen. Set it apart with more than just the bold."*

     It takes the contrast Dan asked for from one channel to two. Weight alone separates the coined
     name from its frame, but it separates it the way any headline separates from any subhead — the
     reader sees emphasis, not a different KIND of word. Colour says the middle line is a name.

     `--bo-primary` and not a new value: it re-solves per theme on its own (#156e97 on light, the
     brighter blue on dark), so the headline follows the same accent as the eyebrow above it and the
     buttons below, and there is nothing here to keep in step by hand.

     THE ACCENT IS NOT SPENT ANYWHERE ELSE IN THIS BLOCK, which is what lets it read as a name
     rather than as a link. The eyebrow is small caps at 11px and the primary button is a filled
     surface — neither is display type in accent ink, so nothing on this screen competes for the
     same signal. If a third accented thing ever lands in the hero, this is the one to reconsider. */
  .splash-title__core {
    display: block;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  /* The meter sits on the headline's baseline at desktop and moves under it when the split
     collapses, where it gets its own air rather than being squeezed.

     It STRETCHES over row 2 now instead of being bottom-aligned in it, because it has two jobs at
     opposite ends of the column: the lockup at the top, level with the headline, and the ladder at
     the bottom, where it has always sat. `align-items: end` on the split cannot do both. The
     instrument keeps its old position to the pixel — `margin-top: auto` inside a stretched column
     lands its bottom exactly where end-alignment landed the whole block's. */
  .splash-meter {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 2;
    align-self: stretch;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin: 0;
    padding-bottom: var(--bo-space-2);
  }

  .splash-meter__instrument {
    margin-top: auto;
  }

  /* THE LAST NINE PIXELS, AND THEY ARE MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED. Row 2 opens at the top of the
     `h1`'s LINE BOX. The top of the headline's first line as the eye reads it is the top of its
     INK, and `.bo-display` sets `line-height: 0.95`, which is smaller than the font's own content
     area, so the two are not the same place. In Urbanist, at the h1's 700 weight, measured with
     canvas `TextMetrics` on the live face:

         ascent                                                          0.950em
         descent                                                         0.250em
         content area, therefore                                         1.200em
         line box (`line-height: 0.95`)                                  0.950em
         half-leading, negative, so the glyphs overhang the box       -0.125em
         tallest ink in "State" (the "t" ascender, above cap height)      0.712em
         -----------------------------------------------------------------------
         ink top below line-box top   (0.950 - 0.712) - 0.125     =       0.113em

     At the 5rem ceiling that is 9.0px, and it was 9.03px when measured in the page. `em` of
     `--bo-t-display`, not a fixed value: the headline is a fluid clamp, so the overhang is too —
     7.0px where the split first appears at 62rem, 9.0px at 1440. A `px` constant would be right at
     exactly one width. It is PADDING and it is positive: nothing here is pulled out of position. */
  .splash-edition {
    padding-top: calc(var(--bo-t-display) * 0.113);
  }

  .splash-meter__caption {
    margin: var(--bo-space-3) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
  }

  /* The air belongs ABOVE the heading, not between the heading and its rail. Three rhythms clear
     of the block before it says "a new section starts here"; half a rhythm under it keeps the
     heading attached to the four cards it names, so the gap does the grouping and no rule or box
     has to. The left edge is not set here on purpose — this sits in the same flow as the eyebrow,
     the headline and the meta row, so the page's one left margin is the only one it can have. */
  .splash-rail-h {
    /* Top is the section seam (`--bo-splash-slide`, one number in `survey.css`); bottom keeps the
       heading attached to the four cards it names, so the gap still does the grouping. */
    margin: var(--bo-splash-slide) 0 var(--bo-space-2);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-24);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .splash-rail {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    /* THE SPLIT STOPS BEING A SPLIT, so the row placement has to be undone explicitly — three items
       all claiming column 1 and rows 1/2/2 would stack two of them on top of each other in a
       single-column grid. Written out rather than left to inherit, which is the same reason the
       theme swaps in this app are written in both directions. */
    .splash-split {
      grid-template-rows: none;
    }

    .splash-eyebrow,
    .splash-head,
    .splash-meter {
      grid-column: auto;
      grid-row: auto;
    }

    /* The 3rem `.bo-split` used to put here as a row gap, back as a margin. See `row-gap: 0`.

       THE RULE MOVED OFF THIS BOX. It used to be this block's `border-top`, which put it ABOVE the
       lockup — so the mark opened the instrument's section rather than closing the one before it.
       Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"move the logo above the line."* It is `.splash-edition`'s bottom border
       now, so the order reads: the ask, the facts about it, the edition this is, THEN the rule,
       then the instrument. The lockup is a colophon on the block above rather than a heading on
       the block below. */
    .splash-meter {
      margin-top: var(--bo-space-5);
      padding-top: 0;
      border-top: 0;
    }

    /* ── THE LOCKUP STAYS IN THE FLOW, AND GETS SMALL ──────────────────────────────────────────
       Stacked, it used to open the meter block at full size: a ~320px logo alone between the fact
       row and the ladder, under a rule, with nothing around it to belong to. Oleh, 2026-08-31:
       *"the Airtable logo looks very odd here"*, and then, after an attempt at moving it:
       *"it should be small and fit clearly into the structure."*

       IT WAS PINNED TO THE TOP-RIGHT CORNER FIRST AND THAT IS NOT IN THIS FILE. Absolute, level
       with the headline, which is where it sits on a desktop. It measured fine at 390 and broke at
       320: the headline is a fluid clamp, its middle line wraps to two, and at that width the text
       ran to x=229 while the lockup started at 173. An overlay over type that reflows is a
       collision waiting for a viewport nobody tested, and "fits into the structure" is the opposite
       of floating above it.

       So it keeps its place in the flow and only changes SIZE. `--edm-ink` is the wordmark's ink
       height and every dimension in `EditionMark` derives from it, so one value takes the whole
       lockup from 24px of ink to 16 — about a third smaller, which turns a logo standing alone into
       a mark labelling the block under it. Nothing can collide with it, at any width, because it
       occupies its own row. */
    .splash-edition {
      padding-top: 0;
      padding-bottom: var(--bo-space-3);
      margin-bottom: var(--bo-space-3);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
      --edm-ink: var(--bo-t-16);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .splash-cta .bo-btn {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
